00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:33.419
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): One word on the state model.

140
00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:34.500
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): So…

141
00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:46.270
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): the vanilla EVM stateDB is organized on top of, storage slots, they're called sources, they're just 32-byte memory chunks, that

142
00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:51.829
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): The smart contract can atomically read or write.

143
00:20:52.210 --> 00:21:05.370
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): And there's these two, bytecode instructions, sload and store, that… that do exactly that. So they load stuff from the given address, or they store stuff to the given address.

144
00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:18.630
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): The EVM underneath has this authenticated key-value store to manage the slots, but we got rid of that, because we don't need it, so we're equipped with this, State DB wrapper, which

145
00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:26.210
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): so we, we don't need the Merkle tree, and we just use directly, fabric,

146
00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:35.269
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): fabric-style KVS. And so we have a naming convention, for instance, EVM, a prefix, just to say these are EVM rights.

147
00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:44.820
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): And then we have the contract address, and then we have the slot key, which is the… basically is this address here that, that these two…

148
00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:53.329
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): operations taken as operands. And then we just do gets and puts, like vanilla fabric gets and puts to these

149
00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:55.829
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): To these storage keys.

150
00:21:56.170 --> 00:22:06.380
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): And obviously, for reads, we're just gonna retrieve what… whatever there was, and we document the fact that we read that at that particular

151
00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:25.480
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): version, because Fabric, as you know, Fabric LedgerDB is versioned. And then for writes, we just don't write immediately to it, but we just, we just record the fact that this transaction wants to perform this write. And as we know, in Fabric, this write will commit only if it's properly endorsed, and there's no,

152
00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:27.709
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): Ledger conflict, and so on and so forth.

153
00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:35.220
