00:46:25.740 --> 00:46:40.510
Marcus Brandenburger (IBM Research Zurich): Do we… would we assume one EVM smart contract per namespace, like in Fabric, or having also multiple names, EVM smart contracts per one namespace? Of course, increasing the risk of

350
00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:47.690
Marcus Brandenburger (IBM Research Zurich): let's say, read-write conflict a little bit more. Is there any… I don't know, is there…

351
00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:54.250
Marcus Brandenburger (IBM Research Zurich): a different issue introduced by using of EVM smart contracts than the standard fabric issues.

352
00:46:54.860 --> 00:47:09.129
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): I would say no, I would say no, because… so ultimately, the only reason to go for a different, for a different namespace versus the same namespace is… is the endorsement policy.

353
00:47:09.790 --> 00:47:17.409
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): For the rest, I mean, this naming scheme guarantees that there's not going to be any, collisions.

354
00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:24.680
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): Nor are there gonna be… Basically, there's no transaction that… no two transactions write into two…

355
00:47:24.830 --> 00:47:35.169
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): Execute in different smart contracts that can touch each other's states, but also that can influence each other's concurrency, and so throughput.

356
00:47:35.350 --> 00:47:44.410
Alessandro Sorniotti (IBM): So ultimately, it's just a question of endorsement policy, really, in my view. I don't know, I don't know if you had a different, different idea.

357
00:47:44.410 --> 00:47:57.690
Arne Rutjes: Yeah, I agree. It's much easier for us right now to keep it in one fabric namespace, and the Ethereum, the EVM, the way it stores it into the slots, the way it creates the keys, already provides all the namespacing we need.

358
00:47:58.040 --> 00:48:08.930
Arne Rutjes: Also, because EVM has features like smart contracts calling smart contracts, that's a lot simpler if we have it in just one fabric namespace.

359
00:48:09.790 --> 00:48:10.500
Marcus Brandenburger (IBM Research Zurich): Right.

360
00:48:11.630 --> 00:48:13.050
Marcus Brandenburger (IBM Research Zurich): Okay, cool, thank you.

